tern-cv:32834f36-a478-45be-97f4-ff2ff51e9f5c prez:description "A layer which is determined by a form of vegetation and is of a distinct height class in the vegetation.",
        "A stratum is an easily seen layer of foliage and branches of a distinct and measurable height. One or more strata can exist in a patch of vegetation." ;
    prez:label "vegetation stratum"@en .

emsa-cv:15361f98-7669-410e-9b04-e9be069c7508 prez:description """
    <h2 id="key-definitions-and-terminology">Key Definitions and
Terminology</h2>
<p><h5 id="table-2">Table 2. Key definitions and terms used in the Vegetation Mapping
Module.</h5></p>
<table>
<colgroup>
<col style="width: 19%"/>
<col style="width: 80%"/>
</colgroup>
<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th>Term</th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Cluster analysis</td>
<td>Numerical analysis of multivariate data (i.e. sites and species) to
assist vegetation classification.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Emergent vegetation</td>
<td><p>The tallest plants in some vegetation communities are so sparse
that they no longer form the dominant or most significant layer. For
example, widely scattered eucalypts or acacias may rise above lower
shrubs or grasses in semi-arid regions. The tallest stratum is then
classified as an ‘emergent layer’ and the dominant layer on which the
vegetation will be classified is usually the next tallest layer.</p>
<p>For example, using the NVIS classification framework, U1 is the
tallest tree substratum. For forests and woodlands, this will generally
be the dominant stratum, except where the substratum cover is &lt;5% and
hence considered ‘emergent’. For a continuum (e.g., no distinct or
discernible layering in the vegetation) the tallest stratum becomes the
defining substratum (National Committee on Soil and Terrain in
prep).</p></td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Foliage projective cover</td>
<td>The percentage of the sample site occupied by the vertical
projection of foliage only (NVIS Technical Working Group 2017).</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Homogeneity measure</td>
<td>A record of the visual estimate (in metres) of the shortest distance
from the vegetation mapping point to a vegetation association different
to the one being sampled (White <em>et al.</em> 2012)(see <a href="#appendix-2">Appendix
2</a>).</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Monitor</td>
<td>Field data collection app for Ecological Monitoring System
Australia. Collects data using the Biodiversity Information Standard for
delivery to the Australian Biodiversity Data Repository managed by the
Department of Climate Change, Energy, the Environment and Water.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>NVIS</td>
<td>National Vegetation Information System. A framework that enables the
compilation of a nationally consistent vegetation dataset from data
collected by states and territories. It provides a comprehensive means
of describing and representing vegetation information based on
establishing relationships between structural and floristic data.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Plant photo voucher</td>
<td>A photo of a plant species clearly indicating its habit, its main
organs (leaves, flowers, inflorescences and fruits) or any other
characters (e.g. spines) that are useful for unambiguous species
identification (see <a href="#appendix-7">Appendix 7</a>).</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Vegetation association</td>
<td><p>A climax community of which the dominant stratum has a
qualitatively uniform floristic composition and which exhibits uniform
structure as a whole (Executive Steering Committee for Australian
Vegetation Information 2003; NVIS Technical Working Group 2017).</p>
<p>Vegetation association (NVIS Level 5) is the recommended minimum
standard for the input of vegetation description data into NVIS. At this
level, the three predominantly recognised strata (where present), are
easily recognised for most vegetation types across Australia. For each
stratum, the characteristic height and cover are recorded, and up to 3
growth forms and up to 3 species (for each stratum) can be used to
describe the vegetation type (NVIS Technical Working Group
2017).</p></td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Vegetation classification</td>
<td>Grouping together vegetation communities that are perceived as
similar in order to simplify the description of the vegetation patterns
within a given geographical area (De Cáceres and Wiser 2012).</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Vegetation mapping point</td>
<td>Geo-defined locations within the project area at which the
vegetation is classified consistent with the NVIS information hierarchy
– to Level 5 (association).</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Voucher specimen</td>
<td>A preserved specimen (whole plant, part thereof, or a tissue sample)
retained for expert identification. Voucher specimens are ideally
deposited in a permanent and accessible storage facility to enable
examination and further study. Refer to vouchering protocols in the
Floristics Module.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>"""^^rdf:HTML,
        """<h3>Contents</h3>
  <ol>
  <li><a href="#module-overview">Module Overview</a></li>
    <li><a href="#introduction-and-background">Introduction and Background</a></li>
    <li>
      <a href="#key-definitions-and-terminology"
        >Key definitions and Terminology</a>
    </li>
    <li><a href="#rationale">Rationale</a></li>
    <li><a href="#equipment">Equipment</a></li>
    <li>
      <a href="#instructions-and-procedures">Instructions and Procedures</a>
    </li>
    <li><a href="#additional-guidelines">Additional Guidelines</a></li>
    <li><a href="#references">References</a></li>
    <li><a href="#appendices">Appendices</a></li>
  </ol>
    <h1 id="module-overview">Module Overview</h1>
<h2 id="available-protocols">Available protocols</h2>
<p>This module provides the procedures and guidelines to classify
vegetation at precise points across the project area. It involves
collecting rapid structural and floristic data within homogeneous
vegetation representative of the visually distinct vegetation
associations present. The data can be used to assist project area
stratification, vegetation mapping, reporting and analysis.</p>
<p>The procedures align with the national approach for collecting
vegetation mapping data outlined in the <em>Australian Vegetation
Attribute Manual: NVIS Version 7.0</em> (NVIS Technical Working Group
2017) and the <em>Australian Soil and Land Survey Field Handbook</em>
(National Committee on Soil and Terrain 2009).</p>
<p>Vegetation mapping data can be collected at any stage of a project,
although collecting it at the start helps to stratify the project area
and inform the location of survey plots, targeted surveys and
intervention activities. Data is entered directly into the Monitor app
and is additional to any plot-derived vegetation data. This module may
be unnecessary if the project area is small (e.g. less than 5 ha) and
the range of visually distinct vegetation associations is already
represented within survey plots.</p>
<h2 id="relationship-to-other-modules">Relationship to other
modules</h2>
<h3 id="mandatory-related-modules">Mandatory related modules</h3>
<p>The Vegetation Mapping Module is not dependent on any other module
having been completed first.</p>
<h3 id="optional-complementary-related-modules">Optional complementary
related modules</h3>
<p>This Vegetation Mapping Module has links to the following optional
modules:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><a href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/d15d05db-5007-411e-b257-105ef4f76821">Plot Selection Module</a> – data from vegetation mapping points may
inform the selection of plot locations. In some instances, plots may be
located at a similar location to the vegetation mapping points.</p></li>
<li><p><a href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/1ff9e97c-3bdd-44c9-bdd3-401fa31c0b32">Plot Description Module</a> – structural formation, vegetation growth
stage, disturbance, and fire history are assigned at vegetation mapping
points as per the methodology adopted for plots in the Plot Description
Module.</p></li>
<li><p><a href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/2b5c2281-e0f4-4ef5-8ae0-8ae745af9a7e">Floristics Module</a> – provides guidelines for the collection of
voucher specimens. Voucher specimens collected from vegetation mapping
points are in addition to those collected within survey plots.</p></li>
<li><p><a href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/c3403517-fcc6-4389-9c09-f1e1ee8b0f3b">Cover Module</a> – if plots are later placed at vegetation mapping
points, the foliage projective cover data collected at vegetation
mapping points can be cross-checked against quantitative cover values
obtained from the Cover Module.</p></li>
<li><p><a href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/6fd39a33-9c4f-469e-80a5-e76b5d5f04a6">Opportune Module</a> – plant species of interest observed at
vegetation mapping points that are not captured by the Vegetation
Mapping Module can be recorded using the Opportune Module.</p></li>
<li><p><a href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/420ce0a7-9364-4bf4-861c-ef5f710e31b9">Targeted Surveys Module</a> – data from vegetation mapping points may
inform the selection of targeted survey locations.</p></li>
<li><p><a href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/8b334ccd-5e1a-474c-88e1-0f9b02d7d9c6">Interventions Module</a> – data from vegetation mapping points may
inform the location of management interventions.</p></li>
</ul>"""^^rdf:HTML ;
    prez:label "Vegetation Mapping Module" .

emsa-cv:26cd531a-8fa1-4bd5-8402-ddb4d41c484e prez:description "A unique sequential identifier given to the vegetation mapping point location." ;
    prez:label "vegetation mapping point ID" .

emsa-cv:ea960328-db99-51fd-843c-61c6c5b6117b prez:description "Refers to the type of vegetation stratum. Upper layer is the topmost layer of the strata, which are further divided into layers, 1, 2 and 3." ;
    prez:label "Upper" .

emsa-cv:f458582f-86fa-53be-b151-c4d7bbee4af2 prez:description "A life stage of plants. For Trees: individuals <2 m height and ~2-10 cm DBH; For Shrubs: has not reached maximum height, apical dominance." ;
    prez:label "plant life stage - sapling" .

tern-cv:b311c0d3-4a1a-4932-a39c-f5cdc1afa611 prez:description "In the context of plant ecology, plant occurrence typically refers to the presence of a single plant or a group of plants of the same species in a specific area.",
        "Plant occurrence can be used to describe the distribution and abundance of plants within a population or community, and it is often used in studies of plant populations and communities. Plant occurrence can be influenced by many factors, such as environmental conditions, competition for resources, and interactions with other species. Plant occurrence is an important concept in plant ecology because it provides information about the distribution and abundance of plants in a given area, which can help to understand the dynamics of plant populations and communities." ;
    prez:label "plant occurrence"@en .

emsa-cv:019f50b1-80ee-537c-97bf-3d1825826a8c prez:description "Life stage of plants representing a mature phase, where fully developed roots, shoots and vegetative compoinents are established and are ready to develop floral traits." ;
    prez:label "plant life stage - mature" .

emsa-cv:0378976a-0018-4604-826d-1886ca5f38c3 prez:description "The proportion of the ground area covered by foliage (or photosynthetic tissue) held in a vertical plane. Foliage Projective Cover is usually expressed as the percentage of ground covered by foliage and can be separated according to vegetation strata." ;
    prez:label "foliage projective cover" .

emsa-cv:4a550407-5158-497c-982a-9f1b662b73e9 prez:description "The growth form of the most dominant/abundant plants at the time the observation was recorded." ;
    prez:label "dominant growth form" .

emsa-cv:5871b3a2-b475-4432-9f15-65424f21a878 prez:description "This concept pertains to the categorization of vegetation into a stratum, which is essentially a division of vegetation into different layers or strata. The NVIS acknowledges these vegetation strata and classifies them into three primary layers: Upper, Mid, and Ground. Each of these strata is further divided into three sub-strata (e.g., U1, M1, G1, etc.), culminating in a total of up to nine distinct layers." ;
    prez:label "stratum type" .

emsa-cv:66aa8058-5e60-5025-a84e-de1234f23d8b prez:description "Woody perennial plant usually of the genus Eucalyptus. Multi-stemmed with fewer than 5 trunks of which at least 3 exceed 100 mm at breast height (1.3 m). Usually 8 m or more." ;
    prez:label "NVIS growth form - tree mallee" .

emsa-cv:6fd39a33-9c4f-469e-80a5-e76b5d5f04a6 prez:description """
     <h3>Contents</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><a href="#module-overview">Module Overview</a></li>
    <li><a href="#introduction-and-background">Introduction and Background</a></li>
    <li>
      <a href="#key-definitions-and-terminology">Key definitions and terminology</a>
    </li>
    <li><a href="#rationale">Rationale</a></li>
    <li><a href="#equipment">Equipment</a></li>
    <li>
      <a href="#instructions-and-procedures">Instructions and procedures</a>
    </li>
    <li><a href="#additional-guidelines">Additional guidelines</a></li>
    <li><a href="#references">References</a></li>
    <li><a href="#appendices">Appendices</a></li>
  </ol>
    <h1 id="module-overview">Module overview</h1>
<p>This module covers the procedures and guidelines for recording
opportune observations of flora (vascular and non-vascular) and fauna
(vertebrate and invertebrate). Fauna observations include sightings and
calls as well as indirect evidence of species presence such as tracks
and scats.</p>
<p>Opportune observations are unplanned (no defined survey effort) and
made while undertaking other activities, at any stage of a project.
Opportune observations often occur while travelling between monitoring
plots and while undertaking other EMSA (Environmental Monitoring Systems
Australia) modules. The likelihood of species detection is increased by
traversing the project area on foot and visiting the range of habitats
present.</p>
<p>Opportune observations are entered directly into the Monitor app.
Minimum data requirements apply to save a record, including taxonomic
name or descriptive field name, location, date and time of the
observation, number of individuals observed, the observation method and
the observer. Additional information can be recorded using the optional
advanced data fields. Records can be accompanied by physical voucher
specimens and/or photo, video and audio files, to verify species
identification. Voucher specimens are linked to a record via a unique
barcode which is scanned within the app.</p>
<h2 id="relationships-to-other-modules">Relationships to other
modules</h2>
<h3 id="mandatory-related-modules">Mandatory related modules</h3>
<p>The Opportune Module is not dependent on any other modules.</p>
<h3 id="optional-complimentary-related-modules">Optional complimentary
related modules</h3>
<p>The Opportune Module has links to the following modules:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><a href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/2b5c2281-e0f4-4ef5-8ae0-8ae745af9a7e">Floristics Module</a> – further details on vascular plant collection
and vouchering methods. Species names should be consistent between these
two modules.</p></li>
<li><p><a href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/1f2ac884-2177-403a-a6c3-27e109b1fc95"> Plant Tissue Vouchering Module</a> – provides standardised survey
protocols for the collection of plant tissue samples and further details
on vascular plant tissue collection and vouchering methods.</p></li>
<li><p><a href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/0771a99f-27f4-4ff7-bf6d-aa70fe22ae51">Vertebrate Fauna Module</a> – provides standardised survey protocols
for vertebrate fauna within plots, and further details on vertebrate
fauna survey, collection, and vouchering methods.</p></li>
<li><p><a href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/0ad98270-707f-4a78-acd1-666faa2c124e">Invertebrate Fauna Module</a> – provides standardised survey
protocols for invertebrate fauna within plots, and further details on
invertebrate fauna survey, collection, and vouchering methods.</p></li>
<li><p><a href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/420ce0a7-9364-4bf4-861c-ef5f710e31b9">Targeted Survey Module</a> – opportune observations of species of
interest (e.g. threatened or invasive) may trigger the need for targeted
surveys.</p></li>
<li><p><a href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/cd04526e-7455-45c1-8a44-55575a4a7b2d">Fauna Ground Counts Module</a> – provides standardised survey
protocols for ground counts of vertebrate fauna (namely pest fauna) and
includes similar data collection fields to those for opportune
observations.</p></li>
<li><p><a href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/8e7c86ce-e283-4cea-aca7-44ed7a22629c">Signs-based Fauna Survey Module</a> – provides standardised survey
protocols for recording signs of vertebrate fauna (namely pest fauna)
within plots.</p></li>
<li><p><a href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/dd2da1e0-edbe-4f20-a1ae-b860876c1837">Herbivory and Physical Damage Module</a> – provides a standardised
survey protocol for recording physical impacts of pest vertebrate fauna
species.</p></li>
<li><p><a href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/f0123418-c565-45a6-be01-c5c332c727e5">Camera Trapping Module</a> – opportune observations of particular species
may warrant the use of camera trapping.</p></li>
</ul>
<h2 id="field-collection">Field collection</h2>
<h3 id="pre-requisites">Pre-requisites</h3>
<p>Pre-requisites for completing this protocol:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Opportune observations can be recorded at any stage of a project
and are not dependent on other modules.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3 id="time-requirements">Time requirements</h3>
<p>Time estimates vary depending on the level of detail recorded and
whether voucher specimens are collected. As a general guide, allow:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>2 – 5 minutes to record an opportune flora observation in the
Monitor app, depending on if a voucher specimen is collected.</p></li>
<li><p>2 – 10 minutes to record an opportune fauna observation in the
app, depending on if a voucher specimen is collected.</p></li>
<li><p>5 minutes processing time per flora voucher specimen (barcode and
press).</p></li>
<li><p>5 – 30 minutes processing time per fauna voucher specimen (e.g.
tissue sampling, preservation, labelling, barcode).</p></li>
<li><p>5 minutes per record to upload any photos/videos/audio recordings
collected outside of the app.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3 id="personnel-requirements">Personnel requirements</h3>
<p>Number of personnel and skills:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Opportune observations can be completed by one person but is
easier with two, so one can make the observations and the other can
record the data directly into the app.</p></li>
<li><p>The surveyor making the observations is ideally familiar with and
experienced in identifying the flora and fauna within the area.</p></li>
<li><p>Scientific permits may be required for the collection of voucher
specimens. Land access permissions are required.</p></li>
</ul>"""^^rdf:HTML,
        """
    <h2 id="key-definitions-and-terminology">Key definitions and
terminology</h2>
<p><span class="anchor" id="table-1"></span>Table 1. Key
definitions and terminology relevant to the Opportune Module.</p>
<table>
<colgroup>
<col style="width: 11%"/>
<col style="width: 88%"/>
</colgroup>
<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th>Term</th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Monitor</td>
<td>Field data collection app for Ecological Monitoring System
Australia. Collects data using the Biodiversity Information Standard for
delivery to the Australian Biodiversity Data Repository managed by the
Department of Climate Change, Energy, the Environment and Water.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Opportune observation</td>
<td>A documented record of a species collected without standardised
field protocol and without an explicit sampling design (van Strien
<em>et al.</em> 2013), associated with a geographic location, date and
time; may be accompanied by voucher specimens, photos, video, audio and
ultrasonic recordings.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Voucher specimen</td>
<td><p>A preserved specimen (whole plant or animal, part thereof, or a
tissue sample) that is retained for expert identification. Voucher
specimens are ideally deposited in a permanent and accessible storage
facility to enable examination and further study. See vouchering
protocols in the <em>Floristics</em>, <em>Plant tissue vouchering</em>,
<em>Vertebrate fauna</em> and <em>Invertebrate fauna</em> modules.</p>
<p>The Opportune Module is not intended to cover the capture or
euthanasia of vertebrate fauna; thus, fauna voucher specimens are
restricted to those collected from deceased animals or observed signs
such as scats and hair.</p></td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Tissue sample</td>
<td><p>For vertebrate fauna, this is a sample of tissue taken from a
live or deceased animal that includes ear clip/notch, toe clip, tail
clip, skin, skin scrape (cetaceans), muscle, liver, heart, blood, hair,
feather, eggshell, scale, faecal sample, egg or semen (Wildlife Ethics
Committee 2020). See tissue vouchering protocols in the Vertebrate fauna
module. The Opportune Module is not intended to cover the capture or
euthanasia of vertebrate fauna; thus, tissue samples are restricted to
those collected from deceased animals.</p>
<p>For flora, tissue samples ideally consist of approximately 10
cm<sup>2</sup> of young, undamaged leaves (or the best/freshest growth
that is on offer) that have been rapidly dried using silica gel granules
to preserve the DNA for subsequent molecular analyses. See also the
Plant tissue vouchering module.</p></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>"""^^rdf:HTML ;
    prez:label "Opportune Module" .

